---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title="How We Track Plugin Prices | PluginDrop"
			description="How PluginDrop's price tracker works: what we track, where the data comes from, what 'lowest tracked' means, and how our deal-score badges are calculated."
		/>
		<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
			"@context": "https://schema.org",
			"@type": "WebPage",
			"name": "How We Track Plugin Prices",
			"url": "https://plugindrop.net/how-we-track-prices/",
			"description": "An explanation of PluginDrop's plugin price-tracking methodology, data sources, and deal-score badges.",
			"publisher": {
				"@type": "Organization",
				"name": "SignalDrop",
				"url": "https://plugindrop.net"
			}
		})} />
		<style>
			.methodology {
				max-width: 720px;
				margin: 0 auto;
				padding: 3rem 1rem 4rem;
			}
			.methodology h1 {
				font-size: 2rem;
				margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
			}
			.methodology .intro {
				color: var(--text-secondary);
				line-height: 1.7;
				margin-bottom: 2rem;
				font-size: 1.05rem;
			}
			.methodology h2 {
				font-size: 1.3rem;
				margin-top: 2.5rem;
				margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
				color: var(--text-primary);
				border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
				padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
			}
			.methodology p {
				color: var(--text-secondary);
				line-height: 1.7;
				margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
			}
			.methodology strong {
				color: var(--text-primary);
			}
			.methodology ul,
			.methodology ol {
				padding-left: 1.4rem;
				color: var(--text-secondary);
				margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
			}
			.methodology li {
				margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
				line-height: 1.6;
			}
			.methodology a {
				color: var(--accent);
			}
			.badge-list {
				list-style: none;
				padding-left: 0;
				display: flex;
				flex-direction: column;
				gap: 0.6rem;
				margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
			}
			.badge-row {
				display: flex;
				align-items: flex-start;
				gap: 0.75rem;
				flex-wrap: wrap;
			}
			.badge {
				display: inline-block;
				flex-shrink: 0;
				font-size: 0.75rem;
				font-weight: 600;
				padding: 0.2rem 0.6rem;
				border-radius: var(--radius, 4px);
				white-space: nowrap;
			}
			.badge-desc {
				color: var(--text-secondary);
				line-height: 1.6;
				flex: 1 1 260px;
			}
			.ds-lowest { background: rgba(230, 57, 70, 0.15); color: #ff8a94; }
			.ds-great  { background: rgba(80, 200, 120, 0.15); color: #50c878; }
			.ds-sale   { background: rgba(255, 186, 69, 0.12); color: var(--sale-text); }
			.ds-wait   { background: rgba(150, 150, 180, 0.12); color: var(--text-secondary); }
			.ds-rare   { background: rgba(150, 150, 180, 0.1); color: var(--text-muted); }
			.callout {
				background: var(--bg-card);
				border: 1px solid var(--border);
				border-radius: var(--radius, 4px);
				padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
				margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
			}
			.callout p:last-child {
				margin-bottom: 0;
			}
			.methodology .stat-line {
				display: flex;
				gap: 1.5rem;
				flex-wrap: wrap;
				margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
			}
			.stat-box {
				background: var(--bg-card);
				border: 1px solid var(--border);
				border-radius: var(--radius, 4px);
				padding: 0.75rem 1.1rem;
				text-align: center;
			}
			.stat-box .num {
				font-size: 1.4rem;
				font-weight: 700;
				color: var(--text-primary);
				display: block;
			}
			.stat-box .lbl {
				font-size: 0.75rem;
				color: var(--text-muted);
			}
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<a href="#main-content" class="sr-only">Skip to content</a>
		<Header />
		<main id="main-content">
			<div class="methodology">
				<h1>How We Track Plugin Prices</h1>
				<p class="intro">
					PluginDrop's <a href="/plugin-prices/">price tracker</a> shows the current price, sale
					history, and deal quality for VST/AU plugins and bundles. This page explains exactly
					how that data is collected and how our "deal score" badges are calculated — so you can
					judge for yourself how much to trust them.
				</p>

				<div class="stat-line">
					<div class="stat-box">
						<span class="num">250+</span>
						<span class="lbl">plugins &amp; bundles tracked</span>
					</div>
					<div class="stat-box">
						<span class="num">2024</span>
						<span class="lbl">tracking history begins</span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h2>What We Track</h2>
				<p>
					For each product on our <a href="/plugin-prices/">plugin price page</a>, we record its
					current price, its typical regular (non-sale) price, its typical sale price, and the
					lowest price we've recorded since we started tracking. We currently follow over 250
					plugins and bundles, primarily sold through <strong>Plugin Boutique</strong> and, where
					relevant, direct vendor stores.
				</p>

				<h2>Where Our Data Comes From</h2>
				<p>
					Price data on PluginDrop comes from a mix of sources, and we label our confidence
					honestly rather than presenting every number as equally certain:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>Automated checks</strong> — a script that periodically checks product pages
						on Plugin Boutique and vendor stores and records the price it sees on that date.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Manufacturer sale announcements</strong> — publicly posted sale prices and
						discount percentages from developers themselves (newsletters, product pages, press
						releases).
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Researched historical data</strong> — for periods before our automated
						checks began, or for gaps between checks, we fill in known prices from publicly
						documented sources: deal-tracking communities, forum posts, and archived sale pages.
						These entries are not something we personally observed in real time — they're
						compiled from public records.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<div class="callout">
					<p>
						We don't pretend every historical data point was captured live. Automated and
						manually-verified checks make up a smaller share of our overall price history than
						researched entries, particularly for older dates. We'd rather be upfront about that
						mix than imply a level of live monitoring we don't have.
					</p>
				</div>

				<h2>What "Lowest Tracked" Means</h2>
				<p>
					When you see <strong>"Lowest tracked"</strong> next to a product, that's the lowest
					price <em>we have on record</em> since we began compiling price history in 2024 — it is
					<strong> not</strong> a guaranteed claim that the product has never sold for less,
					anywhere, ever. Plugin pricing has decades of history in some cases (Waves, Native
					Instruments, etc.), and one-off coupon stacks or regional pricing can occasionally beat
					our recorded low.
				</p>
				<p>
					Where a specific historical all-time low is well-documented — for example, a widely
					reported Black Friday price or a one-time bundle deal covered by multiple sources — we
					note it separately in the product's history rather than folding it silently into
					"lowest tracked."
				</p>

				<h2>What the Deal Badges Mean</h2>
				<p>
					Every tracked product gets one badge, computed automatically from its current price
					compared against its own typical sale price, typical regular price, and lowest tracked
					price. Nothing here is subjective or manually assigned — it's the same formula for every
					product:
				</p>
				<ul class="badge-list">
					<li class="badge-row">
						<span class="badge ds-lowest">At its lowest</span>
						<span class="badge-desc">Current price is at or within ~2% of the lowest price we've ever tracked for this product.</span>
					</li>
					<li class="badge-row">
						<span class="badge ds-great">Great deal</span>
						<span class="badge-desc">Current price is at or below the product's typical sale price.</span>
					</li>
					<li class="badge-row">
						<span class="badge ds-sale">On sale</span>
						<span class="badge-desc">Current price is below regular price, but hasn't reached the depth of a typical sale for this product yet.</span>
					</li>
					<li class="badge-row">
						<span class="badge ds-wait">Wait for a sale</span>
						<span class="badge-desc">Currently at regular price, but this product does go on sale sometimes — it may be worth waiting.</span>
					</li>
					<li class="badge-row">
						<span class="badge ds-rare">Rarely discounts</span>
						<span class="badge-desc">No meaningful sale on record for this product (or its "sale" price rarely moves more than ~5% off regular) — waiting usually doesn't pay off.</span>
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					Because the badge is derived purely from tracked numbers, it can only be as good as the
					data behind it — see the section above on where that data comes from.
				</p>

				<h2>Honesty &amp; Affiliate Disclosure</h2>
				<p>
					PluginDrop doesn't publish fake hands-on reviews claiming we've personally tested every
					plugin we list. Our picks and recommendations are based on published specifications,
					documented pricing, and community consensus (forums, subreddits, and developer
					communications) — not unverifiable first-person claims.
				</p>
				<p>
					Some links on this site are affiliate links, primarily through Plugin Boutique. If you
					buy through one of our links, we may earn a small commission at
					<strong> no extra cost to you</strong>. This never changes which price we display —
					our price data and deal badges are computed the same way regardless of whether a
					commission is attached to a link.
				</p>

				<h2>Corrections</h2>
				<p>
					Prices change, and our automated checks can occasionally lag or miss a change. If you
					spot a price on PluginDrop that doesn't match what you see at checkout, please
					<a href="/about/#affiliate">let us know</a> — we correct reported errors as soon as we
					can verify them.
				</p>
			</div>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
